    "Figure_S4": {
      "caption": "Figure S4. Exploratory bootstrap stability under three sources of variation. R1 resamples test patients at fixed development cutoffs; R2 resamples development patients for cutoff calibration while holding the test cohorts fixed; R3 resamples development and test patients. Each route has 10,000 archived patient-bootstrap replicates with method A, method B, and quality labels kept paired. (A) Method-A external-minus-internal contrasts for H1, low-quality detection (filled circles), and H2, review fraction (open squares). (B) External B-minus-A detection contrasts for the fixed-cutoff policy (dark circles) and equal top-50 review policy (blue open squares). In A and B, points are bootstrap medians and horizontal bars are unadjusted 2.5th–97.5th percentile intervals from the archived valid replicates; a collapsed interval is shown by its point, including the necessarily constant R2 equal-top-50 endpoint. (C) Exact empirical frequencies among jointly valid replicates, with marker shapes identifying four mutually exclusive sign categories. Original strict means fixed B−A >0 and top-50 B−A <0; opposite strict reverses both inequalities; any zero includes either contrast equal to zero; same nonzero means both contrasts share a nonzero sign. Frequencies are not p-values, posterior probabilities, or population probabilities. The observed external fixed-cutoff A-only detection cell is zero: R1 cannot resample an A-only detection and its fixed B−A distribution is therefore structurally nonnegative. Its [0,40] pp percentile interval does not exclude a population A advantage and does not replace the original paired Newcombe interval [−4.28,40.04] pp. Only 16 external low-quality patients inform detection; bootstrap replicates are not new patients. Source: bootstrap_stability/summary.json; no bootstrap was rerun, and original H1–H3 Newcombe intervals are unchanged.",
      "panels": [
        "A: H1/H2 three-route bootstrap medians",
        "B: external policy three-route bootstrap medians",
        "C: exact four-category empirical frequencies"
      ],
