    "Figure_S5": {
      "caption": "Figure S5. Descriptive symmetric Kitagawa decomposition of external-minus-internal differences at the locked development-calibrated cutoffs. (A) Low-quality detection. (B) Review fraction. For each outcome, the total observed difference is separated into a diagnostic-composition-associated term and a within-stratum-rate term using dataset-reported benign and malignant strata. Blue filled circles denote method A, and orange open squares denote method B; points are observed estimates, and horizontal bars are each term's own unadjusted bootstrap percentile 95 % interval. The symmetric formula averages internal and external stratum rates for the composition term and averages internal and external stratum weights for the within-stratum term. Detection weights use low-quality denominators; review weights use all-patient denominators. The two panels use separately labeled horizontal scales. There were 5,000 archived patient-bootstrap draws; detection components have 4,999 valid draws because one draw had an undefined external benign low-quality rate, while detection totals and all review terms have 5,000 valid draws. Observed terms add to their total; percentile-interval endpoints do not have to add. This is post hoc descriptive accounting, not causal attribution or a percent-explained analysis; two diagnostic strata do not remove other confounding. Only 16 external low-quality cases inform detection, and bootstrap draws are not independent new patients. Source: case_mix_decomposition.json; archived estimates and component-specific intervals are unchanged, and no resampling was rerun.",
